# The PS3's motion (#683): each player's motion device, which the console
# makes beside the player's virtual controller (frontend/src/vpad.cpp) for
# RPCS3 to read as its "Buddy Device". A uinput device with the
# accelerometer property, under the virtual controllers' identity (pid.codes
# 1209, product cab0), which nothing else on the console has.
#
# RPCS3 runs as the seat's user, and an accelerometer is the input group's
# alone by default, so this gives the seat's user access, as systemd does for
# pads. After 60-input-id.rules, which marks it ID_INPUT_ACCELEROMETER, and
# 70-uaccess.rules; before 71-seat.rules and 73-seat-late.rules, which turn
# the tag into the grant.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_INPUT_ACCELEROMETER}=="1", \
  ATTRS{id/vendor}=="1209", ATTRS{id/product}=="cab0", TAG+="uaccess"

# The stand-in DualSense (#669, frontend/src/vdualsense.cpp): a /dev/uhid
# device with Sony's id, which the kernel's PlayStation driver turns into a
# gamepad, a "Motion Sensors" device and a touchpad. The gamepad is a joystick
# to udev and gets the seat's access by systemd's own rule; the motion device
# is an accelerometer and would stay the input group's, so Eden's SDL, running
# as the seat's user, could not open it and would pair nothing. The console's
# own name picks it out, since Sony's id is a real pad's too.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_INPUT_ACCELEROMETER}=="1", \
  ATTRS{id/vendor}=="054c", ATTRS{id/product}=="0df2", \
  ATTRS{name}=="CabinetOS player ? Motion Sensors", TAG+="uaccess"
